<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tours</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caesarodunum, Civitas Turonorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tours is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> in west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the historic capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a>. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as Caesarodunum, the chief town of the Turones, it became a great pilgrimage centre after the death of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours">Saint Martin</a> in 397. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> wrote his histories there, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a> led its abbey school under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, and in 732 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> defeated an Umayyad army between Tours and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tours.jpg" alt="Tours" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jules78120 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tram_et_h%C3%B4tel_de_ville_de_Tours.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Gallic times Tours was an important crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the 1st century and was named Caesarodunum, meaning hill of Caesar; by the 4th century the name had changed from Turones to Civitas Turonum and then Tours, and the Tours Amphitheatre was built. Around 380 to 388 it became a metropolis of the Roman province of Lugdunum, dominating Maine, Brittany and the Loire Valley. Its bishop Saint Martin, who shared his coat with a naked beggar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, made the city a major centre of the medieval West on the pilgrimage route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>.</p><p>In the 6th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, author of the Ten Books of History, restored a cathedral destroyed by fire in 561, and Saint Martin's monastery received patronage from the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>. In the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a>, a book collector and abbot of Marmoutier Abbey, made the city a heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-renaissance">Carolingian Renaissance</a>. In 732 Abdul Rahman Al Ghafiqi's army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>, having advanced 500 km into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, was stopped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tours">Battle of Tours</a>. Tours repelled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> attack by Haesten in 845, but the Vikings sacked it in 852.</p><p>Medieval Tours had two competing centres: the City in the east, with the cathedral and the castle of the Counts of Tours, and in the west Châteauneuf around the Abbey of Saint Martin, which became the economic centre after its enclosure around 918. The two were joined in the 14th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> was disputed between the counts of Blois and Anjou, and Anjou won in the 11th century.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a>, who lived at the castle of Montils, Tours was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and he introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry, which still survives. The kings and court stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> until the 16th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> left the private mansions and châteaux of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>. Protestants were imprisoned by the aldermen in the Wars of Religion, which prevented their massacre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tours is the largest city of Centre-Val de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the prefecture of Indre-et-Loire, with 138,668 inhabitants in 2022. From 10 to 13 June 1940 it was the temporary seat of the French government, and German incendiary bombs then burned part of the city centre from 20 to 22 June. Its historical centre is listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tours” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles Martel's victory near <b>Tours</b> and Poitiers in 732 CE checked an Umayyad raid into Gaul.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It ruled through the Siege of Paris, and Gambetta left the city in a hot air balloon to establish the government at <b>Tours</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The mayors of the palace restored it by force: Charles Martel defeated an Umayyad army under Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi between Poitiers and <b>Tours</b>, usually dated 732, and his son Pippin the Short subdued Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian festivals were fitted into the old calendar, the title divus was later given to saints, the Lupercalia was celebrated as late as the 5th century, and the Council of <b>Tours</b> forbade Christians to attend the Parentalia in 567.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The break became clear in the Carolingian age, when reformers restored a classical pronunciation of written Latin: in 813 the Council of <b>Tours</b> ordered priests to preach in the “rustic Roman tongue” or in German, so that the people could understand.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>In 567 the Council of <b>Tours</b> ordered monks to fast every day in December until Christmas and proclaimed the twelve days from Christmas to Epiphany a sacred and festive season.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
